{-
   ==========================================================================================
   C strings - the NUL-terminated byte sequence, and the two conversions across it.
   ==========================================================================================

   The kernel's string type, which is not this language's. A `String` is a run and a live prefix
   (`Text.native.yana` beside this); a C string is an address whose end is a byte in the data, and
   nothing that hands one out says how long it is. Every boundary this library has - the file calls'
   paths, `argv`, the environment, a `getdents64` record's name - crosses between the two, and before
   this file each of them crossed on its own: three scalar NUL scans, two identical `terminated`
   copies and two identical `bytesOf` accessors, in four modules.

   What is here is the set:

   | | |
   | --- | --- |
   | `cStringLength` | how many bytes precede the terminator - one vector pass |
   | `stringFromCString` | those bytes, copied into a `String` this program owns |
   | `stringOfUnits` | the same copy where the caller already knows the count |
   | `cString` | the other direction: a `String`'s bytes with a terminator after them |
   | `stringBytes` | the address of a string's bytes, rooted in the string |
   | `pathTooLong` | whether a path is past what the kernel will look at |
   | `withPathCString` | the two of those for a path, with the copy in the frame |

   **`stringBytes` is the one that has to be written here rather than at each call site**, and the
   reason is provenance rather than reuse. `fn bytesOf(text: String) -> %U8` is accepted in silence,
   and then `openPath(bytesOf(cString(p)), ...)` releases the copy at the accessor call - `freeHeap`
   writes the free list's next pointer over the first eight bytes - and the kernel is handed a path
   beginning with eight zero bytes. `return text` (Analysis-Language.md §1) is what places the drop
   after the call that reads through the pointer, and it has to be said once by a function every
   caller goes through, because a caller cannot say it about somebody else's signature.
-}

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The scan
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   One vector pass: a lanewise compare against zero and a `firstSet` over the hit mask, which is
   `findAscii`'s shape in `Core/Text.native.yana` with the unit pinned at the terminator. What is
   different is the bound - `findAscii` searches a container whose length is known and this one is
   looking for where the length *is* - and that difference is the whole of why the loop is written
   over an aligned address rather than over the caller's.

   **The read is aligned down, and that is what makes it safe on memory nobody promised anything
   about.** Design-Vector.md §5.2's tail-read guarantee covers storage this language allocated, and
   almost nothing reaching here is: `argv`, the environment and a `getdents64` buffer are the
   kernel's, and a pointer out of `Native.bitcast` is whoever's. §5.3 names the case that faults
   rather than merely being unlikely - an object ending exactly at a page boundary with the next page
   unmapped - and a C string is exactly the shape that meets it, since nothing here knows where the
   object ends until it has already read.

   §5.5's align-down argument is what closes it, and it needs no guarantee at all: a `width`-aligned
   load of `width` bytes lies wholly inside one `width`-aligned block, the page size is a multiple of
   `width` on every target here, so the block is wholly inside one page - and that page holds a byte
   the caller says is live. The first block holds `from` itself. Every later block is reached only
   after the one before it turned up no terminator, so the string still runs into it and its first
   byte is live too. **So no load in this loop can fault, whatever surrounds the string**, and the
   price is one `and`, one mask and one subtraction, once per call.

   The lanes below `from` in the first block belong to whatever precedes the string, and a zero among
   them is not this string's terminator - so they are cleared out of the hit mask rather than tested.
   That is done on the *movemask* rather than on the mask register: `bitsUpTo` is one `bzhi` and the
   clear is one `andn`, where `not(maskUpTo(offset))` in the lane domain is a splat, an `iota`, a
   signed compare and an inversion - seven instructions for the same two bits of information, on a
   path taken once per call.

   Against the scalar loop it replaces - the same `while *(text + count) != 0` this library had
   written out three times - at v3, in nanoseconds per call over three million:

   | bytes | scalar | this | |
   | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   | 7 | 1.27 | 0.38 | 3.3x |
   | 16 | 2.90 | 0.38 | 7.6x |
   | 31 | 4.25 | 0.60 | 7.1x |
   | 64 | 13.52 | 0.92 | 14.7x |
   | 128 | 25.36 | 1.29 | 19.7x |
   | 1024 | 115.87 | 7.50 | 15.5x |

   The steady state is four instructions - `vpcmpeqb`, `vpmovmskb`, `test`, `je` - over a vector's
   width, which is where the ratio at the long end comes from; the short end is the head's arithmetic
   against a byte-at-a-time loop that has not got going yet.
-}
pub fn cStringLength(from: %U8) -> Size:
    let width = vectorBytes
    let zeros = zero() :: Vec(U8)

    let offset = (bitcast(from) :: Size) `and` (width - 1)
    let head = bits(vectorAt(from - offset) .== zeros)
             `and` not(bitsUpTo(-1, truncate(offset) :: Int))

    if head != 0 then return (trailingZeros(head) :: Size) - offset

    -- Whole aligned blocks from here on. `at` is the offset from `from` of the block being read, so
    -- the address stays aligned at every step and a hit's answer is a lane index added to it. The
    -- test is the loop's own condition rather than a `break` inside it, so a block costs one compare,
    -- one movemask and one branch and the answer is read off the mask that ended the loop.
    let &at = width - offset
    let &hits = vectorAt(from + at) .== zeros

    while !any(hits):
        at = at + width
        hits = vectorAt(from + at) .== zeros

    return at + (firstSet(hits) :: Size)

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Kernel bytes in, a string out
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The length is found first and the copy made once, rather than pushing a unit at a time: a growable
   string reserves geometrically, so appending `n` units is `log n` reallocations where one
   measurement and one `String.ofCapacity` is none.

   A `%CodeUnit` at the copy and a `%U8` in the signature, which is the same address read two ways -
   what the kernel hands out is bytes, and what a string is made of is this target's encoding units.
   The reinterpretation is free here and is the line `stringBytes` below crosses in the other
   direction.
-}
pub fn stringFromCString(from: %U8) -> String = stringOfUnits(from, cStringLength(from))

-- The same copy where the length is already known - a `getdents64` name, whose scan its caller has
-- already paid for to decide whether the entry is `.` or `..`. Public because measuring twice is the
-- one thing this file exists to stop, and a caller that has the number should be able to say so.
pub fn stringOfUnits(from: %U8, count: Size) -> String:
    let &copy = String.ofCapacity(count)
    String.appendUnits(copy, bitcast(from) :: %CodeUnit, count)

    return copy

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A string out, kernel bytes in
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   The terminated copy, and the accessor that hands out its address.

   **Two functions rather than one**, because the copy has to be *somebody's* - a function answering
   a bare `%U8` would return the address of a run whose only owner was its own frame. So the copy is
   a value the caller holds and `stringBytes` is a borrow of it, which is what the `return` marker
   makes the compiler enforce: `openPath(stringBytes(cString(p)), ...)` places the drop of the copy
   after the call, and a caller who writes the two on separate lines gets the same extent from the
   binding instead.

   `withPathCString` below is the shape to prefer where the string is a path and the block is one
   call, and it is written over exactly these two.
-}
pub fn cString(text: String) -> String:
    let &copy = String.ofCapacity(length(text) + 1)
    String.push(copy, text)
    String.pushUnit(copy, 0)

    return copy

-- The units as bytes, rooted in the string they came out of. The one place the boundary crosses
-- between "the encoding's bytes" and "eight-bit numbers", and free on this target.
pub fn stringBytes(return text: String) -> %U8 = bitcast(stringData(text).bytes.run.items) :: %U8

{-
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A path, without the allocation
   ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

   `stringBytes(cString(p))` is a heap block and a free for every path handed to the kernel, and a
   path is the one string in this library whose length has a *bound*: `maxPathBytes` is `PATH_MAX`,
   and anything at or above it comes back `NameTooLong` whoever allocated the bytes. So the copy can
   live in the frame, which is what this is - the block below the call is where the pointer is live,
   and the lens is what makes that extent the compiler's business rather than the caller's:

       let p = withPathCString(path)
       let handle = openPath(p, flags, mode)

   **The buffer is `@inline(n) @capacity(n) [CodeUnit]` and not `[CodeUnit *n]`, and that is what
   makes the whole thing free.** Both hold their elements in the frame; the difference is what
   *initializing* one costs. A fixed array is a value, so `[0] :: [CodeUnit *4096]` lowers to one
   `setpattern` of four thousand bytes - paid on every call, whatever the path's length turns out to
   be - and against `cString` and its `freeHeap` at 13 ns that priced a `maxPathBytes` buffer at
   19 ns, half again *slower* than the allocation it was replacing. A refined array is a container,
   so an empty one is `alloca` and a four-byte store of the length, and its slots are never written:

       %v_1 = alloca 4100, 4
       %v_2 = add %v_1, 4096
       store %v_2, 0, 4

   That is the whole of the construction at any capacity, so the bound costs nothing and there is no
   size to trade off - which is why this holds `maxPathBytes` where the fixed array had to settle for
   256 bytes and a spill above it. Against `stringBytes(cString(p))` and its `freeHeap`, per call:

   | path | heap | this | |
   | --- | --- | --- | --- |
   | 47 bytes | 15 ns | 7 ns | 2.1x |
   | 400 bytes | 21 ns | 11 ns | 1.9x |

   and neither column of the second row exists for the fixed-array version, which spilled to the heap
   at anything past 256 and was *slower* than the allocation there. Nothing here depends on the
   path's length except the copy itself.

   **`@inline`/`@capacity` will not go on a `String`** - "`@inline` and `@capacity` refine a growable
   array - `[T]`" - so the buffer is the array and the units are appended through `Run`'s own slots
   rather than through `String.push`. That is the one place this file reaches past a container's
   length, and the note at the copy says why it is sound.

   A lens rather than a function answering a pointer, because a function cannot: the buffer is its
   own frame's, and the address would be dangling before the caller read it. A lens's frame is live
   across the block below the call - that is what `withLock` is - so the buffer outlives the syscall,
   and a `return` from inside the block leaves through it like any other exit.

   **No spill arm, and no branch.** A path at or above `maxPathBytes` cannot be opened by this
   kernel, so `pathTooLong` is the answer rather than a longer buffer, and `File` asks that question
   before it asks for a copy - one comparison at the call site against a fallback nobody could reach
   with a path that works. That is what removes the branch this lens used to need, and with it the
   phi whose roots the ownership analysis was losing: there is one buffer now, and one address.
-}
-- Whether a path is past what the kernel will look at, which is the one thing a caller has to check
-- before asking for the copy below. `File` turns it into `NameTooLong`, which is what the `open`
-- would have answered.
--
-- `+ 1` for the terminator, which is why this is a function rather than a comparison every caller
-- writes: `maxPathBytes` counts the terminator and `length` does not, and getting that off by one is
-- how a path of exactly the limit either fails that should not or overruns the buffer below.
pub fn pathTooLong(path: String) -> Bool = length(path) + 1 > maxPathBytes

{-
   The terminated copy of a path, in the caller's frame.

   `values(buffer)` and `copyMemory` rather than `push` per unit: the run's slots are what a kernel
   path is, and the container's own length is not part of the answer - a C string ends at the
   terminator, which is the whole difference between the two. Writing into slots above the length and
   below the capacity is what `String.appendUnits` does one line before it bumps its own count; the
   difference here is that nothing reads the count afterwards, so there is nothing to bump. The slots
   are the frame's, `CodeUnit` has no teardown, and the buffer dies with the lens.

   **`pathTooLong` is the caller's obligation**, and this asserts it rather than trusting it: the
   copy would otherwise write the terminator one byte past a run that refuses to grow. `checkCondition`
   is what every bounds test in this library is and costs a comparison against a constant.
-}
{-
   **`@noinline`, and it is load-bearing rather than a hint.**

   Two reasons, and the second is the one that made it necessary rather than merely right.

   The buffer is `maxPathBytes` of the *lens's* frame. Splicing this body into a caller moves it into
   the caller's frame, where it lives for whatever else that function does rather than for the block
   below the call - and `createDirectory` next door is *self-recursive*, so a copy per level is a
   copy of the buffer per level. A call whose next instruction is a syscall is not worth that.

   And it is what keeps `-inline speed` from running away. `createDirectory` reaches itself through
   the continuation this lens's call site lifts, so the call graph's cycle runs
   `createDirectory -> withPathCString -> continuation$ -> createDirectory`; `findRecursion` in
   opt_inline.cpp computes that graph **once, before the rounds**, on the argument that inlining
   creates no cycle - and `collapsesCycle` then admits one other member of the caller's own cycle per
   round. With the body small enough to take, the rounds do not converge: compiling
   `test/lib/DirectoryWalkSpeed.yana`, whose whole subject is `-inline speed` over `File`, went from
   62 MB and 0.5 s to 1.1 GB and a module arena past the 2 GB its block offsets can address, which
   surfaces as `!(Size(value) & mask)` in `SmallList::set` from `IrEditor::retargetEdge`. That is a
   compiler defect and not this file's - the attribute is what keeps the corpus green while it
   stands, and removing it is the way to reproduce it in one step.
-}
@noinline
pub lens fn withPathCString(path: String) -> %U8:
    -- `4096` written out rather than `maxPathBytes`, because a refined array's bound is a
    -- const-generic argument and a local is not one - "a count is an integer literal or a const
    -- parameter", which is the half of Implementation-Const-Generics.md §10.8 still open, and a
    -- global does not help either, since its own initializer has to be a literal too. `pathBuffer`
    -- in the test file is what checks the two numbers agree: it round-trips a path of exactly
    -- `maxPathBytes - 1` units, which is the longest that fits and the first that overruns a buffer
    -- declared smaller.
    let &buffer = [] :: @inline(4096) @capacity(4096) [CodeUnit]
    let count = length(path)

    checkCondition(count + 1 > maxPathBytes)

    let base = bitcast(values(buffer)) :: %U8
    copyMemory(base, stringBytes(path), count)
    store(base + count, 0 :: U8)

    yield base
